--- machine\system\network\dns
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Dns

Name resolution's machine-wide values, read by resolvd. Everything here
is a fallback or an addition: the live facts — which interface has which
servers and search domains, which carries the default route, which is
exclusive — come from the interface's profile (`Profiles\<path>`, the
`Dns.*` values) by way of netd's control socket, and are never written
here. resolvd reads this key whole on start and on every change.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and Administrators
full, Authenticated Users read).

--- machine\system\network\dns fallbackservers
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Dns FallbackServers
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
applies: live

Servers to use when no interface supplies any — a machine with a static
address and no `Dns.Servers` in its profile, or nothing but a link-local
address. Never consulted while any interface contributes servers; this is
not a "public resolver as well" setting.

--- machine\system\network\dns extrasearchdomains
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Dns ExtraSearchDomains
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
applies: live

Search domains applied to single-label names after every interface's own,
in order, whether or not any interface supplied some. A single-label name
with no applicable domain is `NotFound` without a query; it is never sent
bare upstream.

--- machine\system\network\dns controlsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Dns ControlSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: Everyone: RESOLVER_QUERY; SYSTEM, Administrators: all
applies: live

resolvd's own: the control object's descriptor, self-relative, kept with
the rest of the surface rather than part of the network policy layout.
Checked against the peer's token on every request to
`/run/resolvd/resolv.sock`. Rights: `RESOLVER_QUERY` (0x1) for `resolve`,
`lookup`, `reverse` and `status`; `RESOLVER_CONTROL` (0x2) for `flush`.
Absent or malformed, the compiled default above applies. The stub listener
on 127.0.0.53 is not governed by this object: DNS carries no caller
identity, so that door is anonymous and everything on the machine may ask.

--- machine\system\network\dns\hosts
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Dns\Hosts

Static names: the value name is the hostname, the data an address
(REG_SZ) or several (REG_MULTI_SZ). An exact, case-insensitive match
wins over DNS and is answered at every door — the native socket, the NSS
shim, and the stub listener alike, so a Go binary that speaks DNS itself
sees the same answer. Reverse lookups of these addresses return the name.
This is what `/etc/hosts` was; there is no `/etc/hosts` on Peios.
